        public bool TownDebt = true;                       // town councils borrow and tax: when a town's coffer cannot pay its lord's and the crown's dues, its council borrows what is owed - first from its merchant families and craftsmen (half of what they hold above their savings), then from its notables, last from the Iron Bank - and pays the dues at once. It pays interest and pays the debt back from what the coffer holds above the merchants' stock; the repayments come out of the merchants' profit, so the lord's dues from the town do not rise (and fall at the next review) while the town is in debt. A town in debt slowly loses prosperity. A council that is out of credit raises an extraordinary tax on its own burghers and notables (once every Town Debt Levy Gap Days): it pays its creditors and the dues, but costs loyalty, prosperity and the notables' goodwill towards the lord. Your towns follow the same rules; see Town affairs - The council's debts. Off = no new town debts, as before (debts already owed stand still in the save)
        public float TownDebtRate = 0.10f;                 // with Town Debt: the interest a year a town council pays its merchant families, craftsmen and notables on what it borrowed (0.10 = ten parts in a hundred)
