        public bool HarvestFairs = true;                   // a harvest fair: after the harvest every land holds a two-week fair in its richest town, one land after another from the south to the north (none in a year without a harvest). More buyers come, so that town pays better for trade goods and livestock (not tools or arms) - the money is the town's own, nothing is made from nothing - and every village cart and caravan selling there pays a 1% fair fee to the town's purse (off = no fairs)
        public bool TownGroundRent = true;                 // a third of the rent the working folk and the craftsmen pay for their houses goes to the head of the house that holds the town as ground rent; the other two thirds still go to the townsmen who own the houses (off = all of it to the townsmen, as before)
        public bool CaravanSellWhereDear = true;           // a caravan sells its trade goods where it makes a profit: entering a town it sells, a few pieces at a time and at most half of each stack, every piece whose price there is above what the caravan paid for it plus its share of the caravan's wages for the days on the road. The price counts after the market toll. Goods it carried before the game was loaded are valued at the most the merchants could have paid for them (their worth times the average price level of the realm's towns, or the price in the merchants' own trade log if higher). It sells at a loss only when it has no way out: the goods have lain in its packs for over 30 days, food is going off (over 7 days), it has no coin left for three days of wages, its packs are over their load - and then only as much as brings them under it - or it is being broken up. Unless it is being broken up or has no coin for wages, it sells at a loss only a little at a time: in one town on one day the price level of those goods may fall by at most 30% from where it stood before the first such sale, and no piece goes for less than 0.6 of its worth - the rest waits for the next town. It does not buy back at the same visit what it has just sold, the town pays only from its purse above the merchants' reserve and at most a twentieth of that in a day, and bulk raw goods, animals and horses keep their own rules (off = Banner Kings sells one or two pieces a visit, as before)
