        public bool WorkshopPaceByMarket = true;           // a trade workshop in a town (a notable's or a lord's) works at the pace of its market: more hands where the town has more people than the usual town (at most four times, at least a third), and a little faster where what it makes is dear in that town, slower where it is cheap (the square root of the price, between 0.7 and 1.4); the whole at most four times the usual pace. More cycles take more raw goods from the stall and pay more wages, as if more men were hired. Weapon and armour lines, lines without raw goods, the town's hidden artisans, castles and your own workshops keep the usual pace (off = the usual pace everywhere, as before)
        public bool WorkshopRetoolByPrice = true;          // a notable's trade workshop that has lost money for 60 days or more changes its trade to the one that earns most at its own town's prices after wages - only a trade whose raw goods grow in the town's villages, lie on its stall or come by a materials contract, only if it earns clearly more (by 30%, and at least 10 a day; a trade the town already has counts a fifth less for each such workshop), and the owner pays the new tools to the town's craftsmen from his own purse. At most three workshops a day in the world, one a town in 30 days; a smithy may be chosen (it is the only maker of tools). When a workshop goes bankrupt or is taken from you, the new trade is the best one at the town's prices when it earns a quarter more than the game's pick. Your own workshops, smithies and mines never change by this rule (off = trades change only by bankruptcy, as before)
