        // (NWAR-11..16). Kazdy klucz osobno; wylaczone = jak dotad. Nowe klucze - nie ma ich w Armoury.json Jeffa.
        public bool TownCraftsAfterWorkshops = true;       // the town's own fullers, weavers and tanners (Town Crafts Enabled) work up what is left on the stalls after the town's trade workshops have run their day, not before them: a notable's weavery or tannery, which pays for its flax, hides or wool, its wages and its upkeep, buys first at the morning price, and the town's craftsmen take the rest - they have hands to spare and lose nothing by waiting. The food crafts of Start Calibration keep their place. Off = the town's craftsmen take first, as before
        public bool ClothDemandFromOfftake = true;         // the price of leather and linen in a town (Raw Price By Use) also counts what really leaves its stalls each day for the army's clothing, the smiths' repairs, the villages buying in their market town and the lords' courts (average of about two weeks, read from the goods ledger - needs Goods Ledger Enabled and the log on; with either of them off the measure stops and leather and linen prices go back to the old rule within about a week), not only what the townsfolk and the armourers use: leather and linen are dear where they are worn out, so the tanners and weavers can pay for their hides and flax. Where Start Calibration already prices the villages' basket and the courts, only the army's clothing and the repairs are added there, so nothing is counted twice. Only the price changes - the stock caravans keep and the stall reserves stay as they are. Off = only the townsfolk and the armourers count, as before
